---

<Layout
  title="Yom Tov Sheini: A Study in Halachic Debate | Matthew Miller"
  description="A comprehensive study of the major halachic debates surrounding Yom Tov Sheini Shel Galuyot — especially the question of Israelis observing the second day outside of Israel — drawing on 17 shiurim from leading poskim."
>
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900">

    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="relative overflow-hidden bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-900 via-cyan-950 to-slate-900 text-white">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 50%, #06b6d4 0%, transparent 50%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 20%, #0891b2 0%, transparent 50%);">
      </div>
      <div class="relative max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-20 text-center">
        <p class="text-cyan-400 text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.3em] uppercase mb-4">Halacha LeMa'aseh</p>
        <h1 class="text-5xl sm:text-6xl font-bold mb-3 leading-tight">
          יום טוב שני של גלויות
        </h1>
        <p class="text-2xl text-cyan-200 font-light mb-2">Yom Tov Sheini Shel Galuyot</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300 text-lg mb-8">The Second Day of Yom Tov: A Study in Halachic Debate</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3 text-sm">
          <span class="bg-white/10 border border-white/20 rounded-full px-4 py-1.5 text-slate-200">17 shiurim analyzed</span>
          <span class="bg-white/10 border border-white/20 rounded-full px-4 py-1.5 text-slate-200">11 leading poskim</span>
          <span class="bg-white/10 border border-white/20 rounded-full px-4 py-1.5 text-slate-200">Multiple major debates</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 space-y-12">

      <!-- Background -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="w-1 h-6 bg-cyan-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          Background: What Is Yom Tov Sheini?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-4 ml-7">The origins and contemporary relevance of the second day of Yom Tov</p>
        <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-6 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            In the time of the Sanhedrin, the Jewish calendar was determined by the testimony of witnesses who observed the new moon in Jerusalem. Messengers would then travel to Jewish communities in the diaspora to announce the date of Rosh Chodesh — but those communities far from Jerusalem might not receive the news before Yom Tov arrived. To avoid any doubt about which day was the correct one, diaspora communities began observing <em>two</em> days of each Yom Tov (except Yom Kippur).
          </p>
          <p>
            Even after the calendar was fixed mathematically by Hillel II (c. 359 CE), eliminating all uncertainty, the Rabbis decreed that diaspora Jews should continue observing Yom Tov Sheini — <em>minhag avoteinu b'yadeinu</em>, "the custom of our ancestors is in our hands." This became the defining practice separating diaspora Jewry from their brethren in Eretz Yisrael.
          </p>
          <p>
            With mass aliyah to the modern State of Israel and unprecedented global travel, the ancient distinctions have become pressing contemporary questions: What does an Israeli visiting New York do on the second day of Sukkos? What does a New Yorker visiting family in Jerusalem do? Can you board a flight from Tel Aviv on Yom Tov Sheini? These questions — once theoretical — are now everyday halachic reality.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4 p-4 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/50 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 rounded-lg text-sm italic text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
            "If you're a rav in a Yeshivish community, you will not get the shaila of Yom Tov Sheni. If you're a rav in a Chasidish community, you also won't get the shaila of Yom Tov Sheni. But if you're a rav in a modern type community, then you'll get the question."
            <span class="not-italic font-medium text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 block mt-1">— RIETS Contemporary Halacha 5786</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            One beautiful insight from the shiurim: in Eretz Yisrael, where God's presence is more accessible, one day suffices to fully experience the spiritual power of a Yom Tov. In galus, where that presence is more hidden, two days are needed to reach the same spiritual depth. Yom Tov Sheini is not merely a calendrical compromise — it reflects the existential reality of life outside the Holy Land.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4 p-4 bg-cyan-50 dark:bg-cyan-900/20 border border-cyan-200 dark:border-cyan-800 rounded-lg text-sm">
            <span class="font-semibold text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300">Source note:</span>
            <span class="text-cyan-800 dark:text-cyan-200"> The analysis on this page draws from 17 shiurim sourced from YUTorah, transcribed and analyzed using Sofer.AI.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- MAIN DEBATE: Israelis in Chutz L'Aretz -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="w-1 h-6 bg-cyan-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          The Central Question: Israelis in Chutz L'Aretz
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-6 ml-7">The most contested issue in contemporary Yom Tov Sheini halacha</p>

        <!-- Question framing -->
        <div class="bg-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-xl p-6 mb-6 border border-slate-700">
          <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">The Question</p>
          <p class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug">An Israeli — someone who lives in Eretz Yisrael and has no intention of relocating — visits a diaspora community over Yom Tov. Must he observe Yom Tov Sheini?</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">

          <!-- Lenient: Chacham Tzvi -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border-2 border-green-300 dark:border-green-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
              <div>
                <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-green-600 dark:text-green-400">Lenient View</span>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mt-0.5">One Day Only</h3>
              </div>
              <span class="bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/50 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300 text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded-full font-medium">מיקל</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
              The <strong>Chacham Tzvi</strong> (R. Tzvi Ashkenazi, 1660–1718) holds that an Israeli visiting abroad need not keep Yom Tov Sheini — and more than that, he <em>must not</em> keep it, due to the prohibition of <em>bal tosif</em> (adding to the Torah's commandments).
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
              His logic: the rule of "keep the stringencies of where you came from" applies only to practices you could legitimately continue if you settled permanently. Since an Israeli who permanently moved to Israel may not keep a second day (<em>bal tosif</em>), he cannot keep it even as a visitor. The practice is <strong>place-based</strong>, not person-based.
            </p>
            <div class="p-2 bg-green-50 dark:bg-green-900/20 rounded text-xs text-green-800 dark:text-green-200 italic mb-3">
              "Nireh l'aniyus da'ati d'tzrichim heima l'hisnaheig b'inyanei hamoadim k'echad mibnei Eretz Yisrael hatoshvim — v'ein zeh bikhlal chumrei makom sheyatza misham"
            </div>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-t border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700 pt-3">
              <strong>Also supporting:</strong> Rav Ovadia Yosef (for certain groups), some talmidim of Rav Soloveitchik
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Stringent: Majority view -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border-2 border-red-300 dark:border-red-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
              <div>
                <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">Stringent View</span>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mt-0.5">Two Days</h3>
              </div>
              <span class="bg-red-100 dark:bg-red-900/50 text-red-700 dark:text-red-300 text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded-full font-medium">מחמיר</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
              The majority of <em>Acharonim</em> — including the <strong>Peri Chadash</strong>, the <strong>Nodah BiYehudah</strong>, and the <strong>Mishna Berura</strong> — hold that an Israeli visiting abroad must keep two days privately (<em>b'tzina</em>). The <strong>Beit Yosef himself</strong> (R. Yosef Karo) in his Teshuvos Avkas Rochel considered it a violation of <em>lo tisgodedu</em> for Chutz L'Aretz visitors to form their own minyanim in Israel.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
              The Gemara in Pesachim derives from the Mishnah that one observes the stringencies of both the place they left and the place they arrived — and since the obligation of Yom Tov Sheini travels with the person, it applies even in Israel.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-3 text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-t border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700 pt-3">
              <strong>Supporting:</strong> Peri Chadash, Nodah BiYehudah, Chasam Sofer, R. Akiva Eiger, Mishna Berura, Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, Rav Elyashiv, Rav Tzvi Pesach Frank
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Three Schools of Practice -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-cyan-200 dark:border-cyan-800 shadow-sm p-5 sm:col-span-2">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-cyan-500" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M18 10a8 8 0 11-16 0 8 8 0 0116 0zm-7-4a1 1 0 11-2 0 1 1 0 012 0zM9 9a1 1 0 000 2v3a1 1 0 001 1h1a1 1 0 100-2v-3a1 1 0 00-1-1H9z" clip-rule="evenodd"/></svg>
              Three Practical Approaches (for Diaspora Jews Visiting Israel)
            </h3>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-sm">
              <div class="p-3 bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-900/20 rounded-lg border border-red-100 dark:border-red-900">
                <p class="font-bold text-red-700 dark:text-red-300 mb-1">Majority: Full Two Days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-xs">Daven Yom Tov twice, say Kiddush both nights, do not do melacha even privately. The second day is fully Yom Tov. <em>Rav Schachter:</em> add tefillin without a bracha and listen to havdalah after day one.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3 bg-yellow-50 dark:bg-yellow-900/20 rounded-lg border border-yellow-100 dark:border-yellow-900">
                <p class="font-bold text-yellow-700 dark:text-yellow-300 mb-1">Rav Soloveitchik: "Day and a Half"</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-xs">Me'ikar hadin follows Chacham Tzvi (one day), but respect for the majority → no melacha on day two. Daven tefillas chol, put on tefillin <em>with</em> a bracha on day two, but refrain from melacha.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3 bg-green-50 dark:bg-green-900/20 rounded-lg border border-green-100 dark:border-green-900">
                <p class="font-bold text-green-700 dark:text-green-300 mb-1">Chacham Tzvi: One Day</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-xs">The Chacham Tzvi holds not only that one day is permitted — it is required (due to <em>bal tosif</em>). Melacha is fully permitted on day two. This is the practice of many Israelis and Sephardim.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-3 italic">Note: <em>lo tisgodedu</em> (divisive practices) is a concern in all approaches — an Israeli in a diaspora community who openly does melacha while others observe Yom Tov Sheini may violate this prohibition, regardless of their personal psak.</p>
          </div>

          <!-- Public vs. Private nuance -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5 sm:col-span-2">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-cyan-500" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M18 10a8 8 0 11-16 0 8 8 0 0116 0zm-7-4a1 1 0 11-2 0 1 1 0 012 0zM9 9a1 1 0 000 2v3a1 1 0 001 1h1a1 1 0 100-2v-3a1 1 0 00-1-1H9z" clip-rule="evenodd"/></svg>
              Key Nuances &amp; Distinctions
            </h3>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-sm">
              <div class="p-3 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-700/50 rounded-lg">
                <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">B'Yishuv vs. B'Tzina</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Doing melacha in public within a Jewish community (<em>b'yishuv</em>) is universally agreed to be forbidden due to <em>lo tisgodedu</em>. Doing so privately at home (<em>b'tzina</em>) is disputed: Tosafot forbids it; the Ran, Rosh, and Rambam permit it. The Mishnah Berura rules stringently — even <em>b'tzina</em> is forbidden.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-700/50 rounded-lg">
                <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">In the "Midbar"</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">The Gemara distinguishes: if a Ben Eretz Yisrael is in chutz l'aretz but <em>not</em> in a Jewish community (e.g., at a hospital far from any Jewish neighborhood), even the stringent opinion permits melacha. The prohibition is about <em>appearance</em> within the community.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-3 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-700/50 rounded-lg">
                <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">Intent to Return</p>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">The Ran reads the Mishnah: it only applies to one whose <em>da'ato lachzor</em> — who plans to return to their home community. A clear visitor who plans to go back to Israel does not automatically become bound by the full diaspora obligations, according to some views.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Visitors to Israel -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="w-1 h-6 bg-cyan-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          Visitors to Israel: How Many Days?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-6 ml-7">The diaspora Jew visiting Eretz Yisrael over Yom Tov</p>

        <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm divide-y divide-gray-100 dark:divide-gray-700">

          <div class="p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="mt-1 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-red-100 dark:bg-red-900/50 text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1">Keep Two Days (Mainstream Ashkenazic Position)</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">The <strong>Mishna Berura</strong> and most Ashkenazic poskim rule that a diaspora Jew visiting Israel keeps his full two-day Yom Tov — including private observance of melacha restrictions on the second day. He should not perform melacha publicly. The reasoning: his Yom Tov Sheini obligations travel with him and are not suspended simply because he is visiting Israel temporarily.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="mt-1 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/50 text-green-600 dark:text-green-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1">Keep One Day (Chacham Tzvi's Approach)</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Following the same logic as his ruling for Israelis abroad, the <strong>Chacham Tzvi</strong> would allow visitors to adopt the local Israeli minhag of one day. Since they are present in a makom where only one day is observed, and Yom Tov Sheini is a custom, the local practice governs. This position is held by some Sephardic poskim.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <span class="mt-1 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-yellow-100 dark:bg-yellow-900/50 text-yellow-600 dark:text-yellow-400 flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold flex-shrink-0">½+</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1">"One and a Half" Days — A Practical Middle Ground</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">A nuanced approach discussed in several shiurim: the visitor keeps the second day privately (refrains from melacha) but does not repeat the Yom Tov davening, does not make Kiddush, and does not engage in the full Yom Tov experience. The practical question — whether to join locals for a Yom Tov seudah on what is their regular weekday — is a common real-world scenario addressed by multiple poskim.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 border-t border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-2 text-sm">Rav Schachter's Lo Tisgodedu Principle</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              A fascinating practical ruling: if a person is part of a group and the <strong>majority of that group is following one shittah</strong>, even those with a different personal psak should conform to avoid violating <em>lo tisgodedu</em>. A group that is mostly keeping one day should all keep one day; a group mostly keeping two days should all keep two days. The crucial caveat: this only applies if everyone in the group is truly in the same halachic circumstance. Mixed groups (e.g., some who own apartments in Israel, some first-time tourists) may have enough divergence that <em>lo tisgodedu</em> doesn't require uniformity.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 bg-cyan-50 dark:bg-cyan-900/20 rounded-b-xl">
            <p class="text-sm text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300">
              <span class="font-semibold">Analyzed by:</span> Rabbi Aryeh Lebowitz ("Visitors Keeping Two Days of Yom Tov in Israel"; "Flying Back From Israel on Yom Tov Sheini")
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Flying -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="w-1 h-6 bg-cyan-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          ✈ Flying Scenarios
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-6 ml-7">Modern aviation creates complex Yom Tov Sheini questions that classical sources did not anticipate</p>

        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">

          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🛫</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">Flying Out of Israel on Yom Tov Sheini</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Can a diaspora Jew board a flight from Ben Gurion Airport on what is — for him — Yom Tov Sheini? The question involves multiple issues: is boarding itself an act of melacha? What if the flight was booked before Yom Tov? Does it matter where the flight lands?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              One shiur analyzes this in depth from the perspective that a person keeping one day in Israel (following the Chacham Tzvi) <em>may</em> board a flight — but there is a concern: the moment one leaves Israeli airspace, one arguably should be treating the day as Yom Tov. How does one handle the transition mid-flight?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🛬</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">Flying Back to the Diaspora on Yom Tov Sheini</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              Rabbi Aryeh Lebowitz was prompted to address this when he took attendance in his 11th-grade class on Isru Chag: two boys had gone to Israel for Yom Tov yet were present. One landed at 5 AM; the other at 9 PM — having flown out of Israel on Yom Tov Sheini. "He said the whole plane was full of Jews from the Five Towns, from Teaneck, from Queens... who keep one day in Israel and flew back on Yom Tov Sheni because for them it wasn't Yom Tov."
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              R. Lebowitz's analysis: those following the Chacham Tzvi are technically permitted to fly, but there are serious concerns — most prominently, that one is engaging in <em>uvda d'chol</em> (weekday-type activity on a day that is still Yom Tov for most people around them) and undermining the sanctity of the day.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5 sm:col-span-2">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🚫</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">"One Should Not Board an Airplane on Yom Tov Sheini" — The Territorial Theory</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              A compelling argument from the transcripts: Yom Tov Sheini is <em>territorial</em>, not personal. Just as Shabbos begins earlier in Tzfat than in Teveria based on geography, Yom Tov Sheini depends entirely on one's <em>location</em>. The moment a person leaves Israeli airspace, they are in Chutz L'Aretz — and it is immediately Yom Tov Sheini. There is no personal "carrying" of the status.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              Under this view, someone who boards a plane from Israel on the second day of Yom Tov should expect to be observing Yom Tov conditions mid-flight — including the obligations of <em>v'samachta b'chagecha</em>. "How are you enjoying the chag sitting on a plane? Even with a comfortable seat, more legroom — that's not <em>simchat Yom Tov</em>." The ruling: one should simply not put oneself in this situation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Asking Melacha -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="w-1 h-6 bg-cyan-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          Asking Others to Do Melacha
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-6 ml-7">Complex questions about <em>amirah l'Yisrael</em> and <em>lifnei iver</em> on Yom Tov Sheini</p>

        <div class="space-y-4">

          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-3">Can a Diaspora Jew Ask an Israeli to Do Melacha on Yom Tov Sheini?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              On Yom Tov Sheini, the Israeli is acting on a regular weekday — melacha is completely permitted for him. Yet the diaspora Jew who is keeping Yom Tov Sheini cannot do it himself. Can he ask the Israeli to act on his behalf?
            </p>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-3">
              <div class="bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm border border-red-200 dark:border-red-800">
                <p class="font-semibold text-red-700 dark:text-red-300 mb-1">Majority: Forbidden (Amira l'Yisrael)</p>
                <p class="text-red-800 dark:text-red-200">The prohibition is analogous to <em>amira l'nochri</em> on Shabbos: just as you may not instruct a non-Jew to do work you cannot do, you may not instruct a Jew. The Chacham Shlomo argues it is actually <em>worse</em> — a Jew acts as your <em>shaliach</em>, making you effectively responsible for the act. Authorities: <em>Dover Meisharim</em>, Rav Moshe Feinstein (Igros Moshe OC 3:73), Dayan Weiss (Minchas Yitzchak).</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-yellow-50 dark:bg-yellow-900/20 p-3 rounded-lg text-sm border border-yellow-200 dark:border-yellow-800">
                <p class="font-semibold text-yellow-700 dark:text-yellow-300 mb-1">Rav Shlomo Zalman: Lenient Rationales Noted</p>
                <p class="text-yellow-800 dark:text-yellow-200">Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach was <em>choshesh</em> for the stringent view but acknowledged various rationales why people are lenient. He declined to explicitly forbid asking, yet was concerned about the stringent position. The practical result is that one should not rely on the leniency without a compelling need.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-3 p-3 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-700/50 rounded-lg text-sm">
              <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">What <em>is</em> permitted: Hinting &amp; Israeli Initiative</p>
              <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Two categories are permitted: (1) A vague hint that is not in the manner of a command (<em>beremez she'eino derech tzivui</em>) — e.g., "It's pretty dark in here" is permitted; pointing at a light switch is forbidden. (2) If the Israeli <em>himself</em> offers to help and asks if the visitor wants something done, the visitor may accept — since the initiation came from the Israeli, not the diaspora Jew.</p>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mt-3">Key prohibition also applies if the instruction was given <em>before</em> Yom Tov — even pre-Yom Tov instructions to do work on Yom Tov Sheini are forbidden.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-3">Can a Diaspora Jew in Israel Ask a Local to Do Melacha?</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
              A diaspora Jew visiting Israel — keeping Yom Tov Sheini privately — wants to ask a local Israeli to perform a melacha. An interesting case from one of the shiurim: a woman from chutz l'aretz who is in Israel for Pesach asks a bas Eretz Yisrael to apply makeup on her face on Yom Tov Sheini. Two questions arise: (1) Is amira l'Yisrael permitted at all? (2) Does the fact that the melacha is performed <em>on the body of the person keeping Yom Tov Sheini</em> make a difference?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              Rav Moshe Feinstein (Igros Moshe OC 3:73) addresses whether a Ben Chutz L'Aretz in Israel may ask a Ben Eretz Yisrael to do melacha. The analysis touches on whether the forbidden act is the <em>asking</em> or the <em>resulting melacha</em>, and whether it matters that the doer is acting on behalf of the person keeping Yom Tov. Rabbi Lebowitz's shiur concludes that both questions are interconnected and require careful analysis.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-3">Asking Any Jew: Tosefes Shabbos, Eiruv, Opening Bottles</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              Mrs. Michal Horowitz's shiur addresses a broader range of <em>amirah l'Yisrael</em> scenarios: asking a Jew to do a forbidden act during Tosefes Shabbos (the extension of Shabbos before nightfall), carrying in an eiruv that the asker personally does not use, and opening bottles that the asker does not usually open. These cases explore the limits of the prohibition and whether the benefit accrues to the one asking.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Special Cases -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-1 flex items-center gap-3">
          <span class="w-1 h-6 bg-cyan-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          Special Cases &amp; Edge Scenarios
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-6 ml-7">Eilat, apartments, movers, milah, and more</p>

        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">

          <!-- Eilat -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🌊</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">The Status of Eilat</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              A very contemporary question with ancient roots: is Eilat part of Eretz Yisrael for the purposes of Yom Tov Sheini? The practical stakes: "If you go to Eilat for Sukkot, Pesach, or Shavuot — are you keeping one day or two? The nafka mina: could you go snorkeling on Yom Tov Sheini?"
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              Eilat lies south of the borders of Eretz Yisrael as defined by many halachic authorities. If it is technically <em>chutz la'aretz</em>, then Israeli residents of Eilat would be obligated in Yom Tov Sheini, and diaspora visitors could not consider themselves in the Eretz Yisrael framework at all.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach develops an interesting <em>svara</em>: the relevant factor may be whether a Jewish government actually controls the territory. Since the State of Israel has sovereign control over Eilat, there is an argument that it is legally treated as part of Eretz Yisrael for these purposes — even if it's outside the biblical borders. The shiur works through the geographic, political, and halachic dimensions of this question.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Apartments -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🏠</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">Owning an Apartment in Israel</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              A growing contemporary question: many diaspora Jews own apartments in Israel, visiting regularly but maintaining their primary lives elsewhere. Rabbi Lebowitz was motivated to address this by his own parents' situation — they purchased an apartment in Israel, "and that opened up a whole new series of shailos."
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              The fundamental question: does owning an apartment make one a <em>ben Eretz Yisrael</em>? The shiur builds on a teshuvah by <strong>Rav Asher Weiss</strong> (published in <em>Yeshurun</em> journal / Minchas Asher), who traces the question through a surprising source: the Teshuvos Maharit Tzalon (Siman 52), which discusses a man with two wives — one in Eretz Yisrael and one in chutz l'aretz — asking which Yom Tov schedule he follows. The answer: it depends on where his <em>primary</em> residence is.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              The parallel: an apartment owner who visits Israel periodically but lives mainly in America has their <em>primary</em> residence in chutz l'aretz and remains a full ben chutz l'aretz. Ownership alone does not confer a change in status — it depends on where one genuinely lives.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Movers -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">📦</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">Minhag Change When Moving: YD 115:3</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              The <strong>Rama</strong> in Yoreh De'ah 115:3 discusses how customs — including dairy-related practices — change when a person moves to a new community. Rabbi Yaacov Thaler's shiur applies this framework specifically to Yom Tov Sheini: when someone moves from Israel to the diaspora (or vice versa), at what point do their Yom Tov Sheini obligations change?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              The Rama's principle is that one adopts stringencies of both the old and new location during the period of transition — which has significant implications for someone in the process of making aliyah or leaving Israel.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Milah -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">✡️</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">Milah on Yom Tov Sheini (YD 266:8)</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              A <em>Mila b'zmanah</em> (on the eighth day) overrides Shabbat and Yom Tov. But what about <em>mila shelo bizmana</em> — a bris scheduled for after the eighth day due to illness or other delay — when it falls on Yom Tov Sheini?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              A fundamental machlokes: The <strong>Rambam</strong> holds that <em>mila shelo bizmana</em> <em>is</em> docha Yom Tov Sheini (overrides it). The <strong>Rosh</strong> disagrees — it does not override Yom Tov Sheini. This is described in Rabbi Thaler's shiur as "a major-league machlokes Rambam-Rosh."
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              The underlying question reflects the fundamental debate about Yom Tov Sheini's status: if it is treated as a full rabbinic Yom Tov, then mila shelo bizmana — which does not override the Torah's own Yom Tov — should not override a rabbinic one either (Rosh). But the Rambam's view suggests Yom Tov Sheini has a softer status that a deferred mila can override.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Accepting Early -->
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 shadow-sm p-5 sm:col-span-2">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🕯️</span>
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100">Accepting Shavuos Early — Effect on Yom Tov Sheini</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              A fascinating edge case: On Shavuos, the Magen Avraham rules that one must wait until <em>tzeis hakochavim</em> (nightfall) to make Kiddush, because the Torah requires <em>temimos</em> — seven <em>complete</em> weeks of Sefiras HaOmer. If Shavuos is accepted early, before the full seven weeks are technically complete, is the acceptance valid? And if someone accepts Yom Tov before nightfall, does this affect when Yom Tov Sheini begins?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-2">
              The shiur explores how Tosefes Yom Tov — the voluntary extension of Yom Tov into the preceding night — interacts with the calendar-based structure of Yom Tov Sheini. If accepting Shabbos early makes a <em>personal</em> difference in when Shabbos "starts" for an individual, does the same mechanism apply to a communal or individual acceptance of Yom Tov on Shavuos?
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
              The deeper issue is whether Yom Tov Sheini is purely calendar-determined (and thus cannot be shifted by personal acceptance) or whether there is an element of <em>kabbalah</em> that could affect its timing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

